# Erdős Problem #99 — the sharp loaded annulus is an exact piecewise translation of the unit shell

**Date:** 2026-09-09  
**Repository branch:** `research/fullcore-interface-perimeter-2026-09-09`  
**Run type:** exact local geometry / phase-interface reduction  
**Target:** full-core near-circle branch with `Q_B/D->2`  
**Depends on:** AS annular saturation, CF central-face concentration, C51 convex bounded faces  
**Computation:** not used

## LEVEL I STATUS

`STILL OPEN`

AS shows that in the sharp interface branch all but `o(D)` loaded terminals have exactly two loaded shell contacts and one unique interior contact, and all but `o(D)` shell-facing bounded faces have exactly one shell side and two loaded endpoints. CF further shows that the total defect of the regular shell-annular faces is `o(D)`.

For a regular shell edge `b_i b_{i+1}`, let the unique unloaded neighbors of its endpoints be `u_i,u_{i+1}`. If the adjacent bounded interface face is a quadrilateral, its boundary is

\[
b_i,b_{i+1},u_{i+1},u_i.
\]

All four boundary edges are unit contacts. Therefore the face is a nondegenerate rhombus, hence a parallelogram, and

\[
\boxed{
u_i-b_i=u_{i+1}-b_{i+1}.}
\tag{PT.1}

Thus the spoke vector

\[
w_i:=u_i-b_i,
\qquad |w_i|=1,
\]

is exactly constant along every consecutive run of quadrilateral annular faces.

A change of spoke vector can occur only across

1. an exceptional annular face;
2. an irregular loaded terminal/shell interval;
3. a harmonic-component label transition.

In the `Q_B/D->2` branch the first two classes have total cardinality `o(D)`, and AS puts the third class inside the same `o(D)` exceptional shell set. Hence:

### Theorem PT — piecewise-translation annulus

After deleting `o(D)` loaded shell vertices/edges, the remaining near-complete unit shell decomposes into disjoint cyclic runs on each of which

\[
\boxed{u_i=b_i+w}
\tag{PT.2}

for one fixed unit vector `w` depending only on the run.

Equivalently, away from `o(D)` phase-jump locations, the inner boundary chain of the harmonic contact complex is an **exact Euclidean translate** of the outer loaded unit chain.

Moreover triangle-freeness gives a local phase interval. If

\[
e_i:=b_{i+1}-b_i,
\qquad |e_i|=1,
\]

then the two rhombus diagonals are `e_i+w` and `e_i-w`. Both are noncontact pairs and therefore have length strictly greater than one. Hence

\[
|e_i-w|^2=2-2e_i\cdot w>1,
\]

\[
|e_i+w|^2=2+2e_i\cdot w>1,
\]

so

\[
\boxed{-\frac12<e_i\cdot w<\frac12.}
\tag{PT.3}

Equivalently, the oriented shell-side direction on one translation run stays strictly between `60` and `120` degrees from its fixed spoke vector.

PT is the exact shell analogue of a rhombic grain phase. It is stronger than saying that the annulus is “near rhombic”: on each surviving run the translation/spoke phase is literally constant.

The result does not bound the number of runs by a constant. There are only `o(D)` jump locations, but a near-disk convex boundary can concentrate a nonzero total turning angle on a sublinear set of vertices, so no unjustified curvature-equipartition is used.

---

## 1. Regular quadrilateral interface face

Take a shell edge `b_i b_{i+1}` satisfying all AS regularity conditions:

- it is a direct unit loaded-loaded hull contact;
- both endpoints have their unique interior spokes;
- the bounded face on the interior side has no additional loaded terminal.

AS proves that the face boundary is

\[
b_i\to b_{i+1}\to u_{i+1}\leadsto u_i\to b_i,
\]

where the internal `u_{i+1}`-to-`u_i` contact path has length `L_i>=1` and

\[
Q_F=L_i-1.
\]

Thus `Q_F=0` exactly when `L_i=1`, i.e. exactly when the face is the four-cycle

\[
b_i,b_{i+1},u_{i+1},u_i.
\]

All four sides are active unit contacts.

A Euclidean quadrilateral with all four sides equal is a parallelogram: the diagonal joining opposite vertices splits it into two congruent SSS triangles. Therefore opposite sides are equal as vectors, yielding PT.1.

---

## 2. Changes of spoke phase are defect-supported

Suppose two consecutive regular shell faces sharing the loaded terminal `b_{i+1}` are both quadrilateral. PT.1 for the first gives

\[
u_i-b_i=u_{i+1}-b_{i+1},
\]

and for the second gives

\[
u_{i+1}-b_{i+1}=u_{i+2}-b_{i+2}.
\]

Thus the spoke vector is identical across both faces. Induction gives one constant vector on an entire quadrilateral run.

Therefore `w` can change only where one of the hypotheses fails.

AS gives only `o(D)` irregular loaded terminals and only `o(D)` shell edges at which the harmonic-component label changes. CF proves that regular annular face defect is `o(D)`. Every exceptional regular annular face contributes at least one to that defect, so there are only `o(D)` such faces.

Delete the union of these exceptional locations. Its size is `o(D)`, and every remaining connected shell run satisfies PT.2.

---

## 3. Triangle-free phase interval

On one quadrilateral face write

\[
e=b_{i+1}-b_i,
\qquad
w=u_i-b_i=u_{i+1}-b_{i+1}.
\]

Then the two diagonals are

\[
u_i-b_{i+1}=w-e,
\]

and

\[
u_{i+1}-b_i=w+e.
\]

If either diagonal had length one, one diagonal endpoint together with either adjacent face vertex would form a unit equilateral triangle because the two incident rhombus sides are already unit. More directly, every source pair has distance at least one and triangle-freeness makes both diagonal inequalities strict:

\[
|w-e|>1,
\qquad
|w+e|>1.
\]

Since `|e|=|w|=1`, squaring gives PT.3.

Thus if `theta_i` is the oriented angle from `e_i` to `w` chosen in `(0,pi)`,

\[
\boxed{\frac\pi3<\theta_i<\frac{2\pi}{3}.}
\tag{3.1}

On a fixed translation run, `w` is constant while the convex shell-side direction turns monotonically. Consequently the total side-direction turn inside one run is strictly less than `pi/3`.

This last fact is exact for a run. It does **not** imply a linear lower bound on the number of phase jumps, because the deleted `o(D)` shell locations may carry non-negligible total turning in a near-disk sequence.

---

## 4. Dominant semicircle blocks

FS/CF gives two dominant harmonic components attached along complementary shell blocks, up to `o(D)` exceptional terminals. PT therefore applies separately along almost all of each dominant block.

Inside each block, the annular inner boundary is obtained from the loaded outer shell by a sequence of exact unit translations. Between translation runs, exceptional interface faces act as discrete phase slips.

This gives a concrete dictionary with the Target-B rhombic phase language:

```text
loaded shell side direction e_i
+ unique interior spoke w
+ quadrilateral annular face
    <=> exact unit rhombus with phase (e_i,w)

same w on consecutive quadrilaterals
    <=> coherent boundary rhombic grain

exceptional annular face / irregular terminal
    <=> phase-slip location.
```

Unlike the bulk Target-B grain decomposition, no asymptotic angular approximation is needed for constancy of `w` on a run.

---

## 5. Why PT is not already a contradiction

A fixed spoke vector can support a shell-side direction interval of width almost `pi/3` while maintaining strict triangle-freeness. A half-circle of shell turning can therefore be covered by finitely many long translation runs separated by finitely many pentagonal or higher interface faults.

Such a construction is consistent with the known five-sector / oblique-rhombic regressions. Therefore one cannot infer `Omega(D)` interface defect merely from the total `pi` turning of one semicircular shell block.

The useful new information is instead that every phase change has an identifiable exceptional-face carrier. A future winding/phase theorem can charge changes to the actual face defect rather than to an abstract grain decomposition.

---

## 6. Interaction with the central face

CF shows that the inward outer boundary of each dominant harmonic component is one near-geodesic path of length `D+o(D)`, and the two such paths form the boundary of the single central exceptional face.

Thus each dominant component has a closed boundary made of

- a shell-facing piecewise-translation arc governed by PT;
- one near-straight central contact path.

The natural phase target is now finite and explicit: transport the spoke/edge direction through the component's stopped-train system from the shell translation runs to the central near-geodesic path. If strict rhombic phase can be transported without enough internal exceptional faces, one of the endpoint rhombi must hit the forbidden `60`-degree value.

EP shows that there are in fact many internal faults (`Q_int>=0.990D` and at least `0.763D` pentagons), so any successful transport theorem must use their endpoint budget rather than assume a coherent disk-wide grain.

---

